1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,000
Egyptian mummies.

2
00:00:02,001 --> 00:00:06,000
These ancient preserved bodies are speaking from the dead.

3
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Computer scanning virtually unwraps them to reveal their secrets.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Was King Tut murdered? Technology has the answer.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Learn how to make a mummy and how to preserve one for millennia.

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now, Mummy Tech on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
This patient is about to receive a dose of high-technology treatment.

8
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:51,440
Start the scan.

9
00:00:51,441 --> 00:01:02,620
Computer tomography scanning, known as CT or CAT scanning, explores inside a human body

10
00:01:02,621 --> 00:01:06,460
and is a life-saving tool for diagnosing disease.

11
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:10,800
But in this case, the patient is dead.

12
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:13,720
In fact, dead for 5,000 years.

13
00:01:13,721 --> 00:01:20,760
But hopefully the CT scan will tell us why he died and what conditions may have led to his death.

14
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:23,611
This mummy from the Michael C.

15
00:01:23,612 --> 00:01:30,920
Carlos Museum in Atlanta, Georgia, is examined without being unwrapped to uncover the secrets inside.

16
00:01:33,020 --> 00:01:37,760
Looks like there is some tissue there. Wow. Oh, that's really interesting.

17
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:41,880
CT scanning is based on the X-ray principle.

18
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:50,020
As X-rays pass through the body, they're absorbed at differing levels, creating a flat image on film.

19
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:59,260
In the case of the CT, the x-rays are fired from various angles around the subject to

20
00:01:59,261 --> 00:02:03,060
create a cross-section, or slice.

21
00:02:03,061 --> 00:02:08,280
As the patient moves through the scanner, up to 1,500 slices are produced and stacked

22
00:02:08,281 --> 00:02:14,100
together like a deck of cards to form a comprehensive picture of the body.

23
00:02:14,101 --> 00:02:20,420
Technology has really revolutionized both the study of mummies and dealing with them

24
00:02:20,421 --> 00:02:24,020
because it helps us not only understand how they were made,

25
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:28,660
but it helps us preserve them for future generations.

26
00:02:28,760 --> 00:02:30,160
Across the country,

27
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:34,960
at the Rosicrucian Egyptian Museum in San Jose, California,

28
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:38,580
a curiously tiny mummy has been on display

29
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:42,780
for more than 75 years.

30
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:44,880
The mystery of this small mummy

31
00:02:44,980 --> 00:02:48,320
is a story of who this was,

32
00:02:48,321 --> 00:02:53,560
was how this person lived and how this person died.

33
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:57,600
And given that this is a small mummy, the assumption was a child.

34
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:01,020
To unlock the mystery beneath the bandages,

35
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:06,140
Dr. Amy Ladd, along with a team of scanning and computer experts,

36
00:03:07,480 --> 00:03:10,700
performed one of the most sophisticated high-tech medical exams

37
00:03:10,701 --> 00:03:13,440
ever carried out on an Egyptian mummy.

38
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:16,300
This technology is fascinating.

39
00:03:16,301 --> 00:03:18,240
It's like doing a virtual autopsy.

40
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:20,320
without touching the mummy at all.

41
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:25,620
The type of scanner used on the mummy is called a C-arm.

42
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:30,240
It's a movable arm that completely encircles the subject.

43
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:36,320
The C-arm produces an astounding 60,000 image slices,

44
00:03:36,860 --> 00:03:41,300
which deliver details rarely before seen in a mummy scan.

45
00:03:42,860 --> 00:03:44,800
This is breakthrough technology.

46
00:03:44,801 --> 00:03:48,380
Humans cannot even go through the scanner right now.

47
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:49,340
It's that new.

48
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:52,220
The thousands of images from the scanner

49
00:03:52,221 --> 00:03:54,000
were then fed into a supercomputer

50
00:03:54,001 --> 00:03:59,140
to create a virtual three-dimensional image inside the mummy.

51
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:05,800
It's a fantastic thing because it's preserved in its original state.

52
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:08,520
And as we found out, it is preserved so well

53
00:04:08,521 --> 00:04:10,620
that we can see details as if it were a child

54
00:04:10,621 --> 00:04:12,680
that we're seeing in the clinic today.

55
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:15,640
With these astounding images,

56
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:19,460
Dr. Ladd was able to solve the mystery of the tiny mummy.

57
00:04:20,780 --> 00:04:24,300
First, by examining the 3,000-year-old teeth.

58
00:04:26,080 --> 00:04:28,180
This is the mummy scan of the jaw.

59
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:29,480
This is from the side.

60
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:34,900
You see the lower jaw, baby teeth, which are already in place,

61
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:38,940
and the teeth that haven't come in yet that are beginning to form,

62
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:40,880
including the six-year molars.

63
00:04:40,881 --> 00:04:44,640
This tells us the age because there's baby teeth still in place,

64
00:04:44,740 --> 00:04:47,980
so we know it's a young child, hasn't lost any teeth.

65
00:04:48,460 --> 00:04:50,200
The six-year molars haven't come in yet,

66
00:04:50,240 --> 00:04:52,380
which tells that this child is younger than six.

67
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:58,980
Details from under the wrappings also revealed clues about the sex of the child.

68
00:05:00,660 --> 00:05:04,920
So this image of the mummy skull, the child's skull,

69
00:05:05,220 --> 00:05:08,740
we know it's a girl because on the skull there is a wavy pattern,

70
00:05:08,741 --> 00:05:11,160
which is the preservation of the child's hair.

71
00:05:11,320 --> 00:05:15,420
And at that time, a girl had hair and a boy had his head shaved.

72
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:21,140
We also know it's a girl because there is no mummified genitalia between the legs,

73
00:05:21,340 --> 00:05:23,620
which, by default, that's a girl and not a boy.

74
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:29,800
The scanning images were even able to tell the story of how the little girl may have died.

75
00:05:32,300 --> 00:05:37,200
There's a suggestion of there's some scalloping out these kind of grayish areas,

76
00:05:37,201 --> 00:05:41,300
which possibly means there was increased intracranial pressure,

77
00:05:41,460 --> 00:05:44,820
meaning this child may have had a virus or a bacteria infection

78
00:05:44,821 --> 00:05:47,880
causing swelling in the brain, which may have been the cause of death.

79
00:05:50,440 --> 00:05:52,200
So it was really a tragedy.

80
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:54,960
There was this healthy child probably running around

81
00:05:54,961 --> 00:05:56,720
until just a few weeks before her death.

82
00:05:57,160 --> 00:05:58,640
And what an abrupt ending.

83
00:05:58,641 --> 00:06:02,640
This is a kid who had a whole life to live, which wasn't.

84
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:05,000
And it's preserved in time,

85
00:06:05,001 --> 00:06:08,400
and that's been a wonderful experience, unraveling that mystery.

86
00:06:10,760 --> 00:06:14,040
Our high tech may help us understand the past,

87
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:17,700
but how did the Egyptians use their technology

88
00:06:17,701 --> 00:06:22,600
to accomplish some of the most amazing body preservation the world has ever known?

89
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:25,900
The Egyptians were the techies of their day.

90
00:06:26,540 --> 00:06:28,600
They had skills that nobody else had.

91
00:06:29,240 --> 00:06:32,320
Dr. Robert Breyer, a renowned Egyptologist,

92
00:06:32,321 --> 00:06:38,780
is considered by many to be the world's foremost expert on mummies and the mummification process.

93
00:06:39,780 --> 00:06:40,480
In fact...

94
00:06:41,380 --> 00:06:44,220
People sometimes call me Mr. Mummy. I'd never call myself that.

95
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:47,940
But I think it's that they're surprised that there's anybody who would work with just mummies.

96
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,240
Dr. Breyer is so fascinated by mummies,

97
00:06:51,780 --> 00:06:55,240
he wanted to accomplish what no one else had in thousands of years.

98
00:06:55,241 --> 00:07:05,260
In 1994, he performed and documented an actual body mummification, using the same process

99
00:07:05,261 --> 00:07:06,420
as the ancient Egyptians.

100
00:07:06,421 --> 00:07:13,120
I decided to mummify a human cadaver because I wanted to know exactly how the Egyptians

101
00:07:13,121 --> 00:07:14,160
mummified their dead.

102
00:07:15,160 --> 00:07:18,562
Without the use of modern medical technology, Dr.

103
00:07:18,563 --> 00:07:21,200
Breyer set out to mummify a body using

104
00:07:21,201 --> 00:07:24,680
the exact tools available to the Egyptians millennia ago.

105
00:07:25,240 --> 00:07:29,380
In order to do the project, we had to replicate Egyptian tools.

106
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:33,680
Now, they had knives made out of bronze and copper,

107
00:07:33,960 --> 00:07:37,360
but they also had obsidian, which is volcanic glass,

108
00:07:37,580 --> 00:07:40,860
and that's very sharp, even sharper than surgical scalpels.

109
00:07:41,080 --> 00:07:42,720
So they used that for incisions, too.

110
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:45,720
Using a cadaver donated to science,

111
00:07:46,360 --> 00:07:50,300
Dr. Breyer began his first step in the Egyptian mummification process,

112
00:07:51,540 --> 00:07:53,420
removing the internal organs,

113
00:07:53,421 --> 00:07:55,380
which are prone to rapid decay.

114
00:07:57,620 --> 00:07:59,220
In order to preserve a body,

115
00:07:59,620 --> 00:08:02,040
you have to make sure that bacteria don't act on it.

116
00:08:02,340 --> 00:08:03,600
Bacteria is what causes decay.

117
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:05,260
They act on the soft tissue.

118
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:08,540
And the reason they go to the soft tissue is there's water there.

119
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:11,940
So the key is dehydrate quickly, get rid of the moisture,

120
00:08:12,180 --> 00:08:13,960
so the bacteria won't decay your body.

121
00:08:14,660 --> 00:08:16,840
Now, this tool was really important.

122
00:08:17,720 --> 00:08:20,320
They removed the internal organs through a tiny incision,

123
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:21,880
only 3 1⁄2 inches wide.

124
00:08:21,881 --> 00:08:24,460
So you can't get your full hands, you can't get two hands in.

125
00:08:24,760 --> 00:08:27,220
So this tool, when you've got your incision made,

126
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:30,540
you can put it in the incision and kind of pull the organs out.

127
00:08:31,740 --> 00:08:34,320
The Egyptians made such small incisions

128
00:08:34,321 --> 00:08:37,940
because they wanted to cause bodies as little damage as possible.

129
00:08:41,420 --> 00:08:44,340
Ancient embalmers removed all the internal organs,

130
00:08:44,780 --> 00:08:50,240
from the stomach and lungs to the liver, pancreas and gallbladder.

131
00:08:50,241 --> 00:08:53,240
The only organ left was the heart.

132
00:08:53,340 --> 00:08:55,240
They left the heart in place

133
00:08:55,340 --> 00:08:57,840
because they believed you thought with your heart.

134
00:08:57,940 --> 00:08:59,840
They didn't realize you thought with your brain.

135
00:08:59,940 --> 00:09:02,280
So they left the heart in place because you're going to need that

136
00:09:02,380 --> 00:09:04,980
to do your thinking when you get to the next world.

137
00:09:05,080 --> 00:09:07,300
Once the organs were removed,

138
00:09:07,400 --> 00:09:11,200
the Egyptians stored them and buried them with the mummy

139
00:09:11,300 --> 00:09:15,200
in containers called canopic jars.

140
00:09:15,300 --> 00:09:16,900
It's really a jar.

141
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:17,900
It's got a lid.

142
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,600
It's hollow inside.

143
00:09:19,601 --> 00:09:23,500
And the lid is in the shape of a god who protects the internal organs.

144
00:09:24,180 --> 00:09:28,340
And the ancient Egyptians put the internal organs inside for protection.

145
00:09:29,580 --> 00:09:32,220
Nearly morticians didn't preserve the brain

146
00:09:32,221 --> 00:09:35,800
because they thought it was responsible for producing mucus.

147
00:09:36,560 --> 00:09:38,880
So to keep skull damage to a minimum,

148
00:09:39,980 --> 00:09:42,600
they removed the brain through the nose.

149
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:47,160
Undoubtedly, the hardest thing for the Egyptians to do was remove the brain.

150
00:09:47,161 --> 00:09:49,160
Now, this was done by a skilled surgeon,

151
00:09:49,161 --> 00:09:52,160
somebody who really knew what he was doing.

152
00:09:52,161 --> 00:09:55,160
The surgeon opened a pathway to the brain

153
00:09:55,161 --> 00:09:57,160
by breaking through the ethmoid bone

154
00:09:57,161 --> 00:10:00,160
in the back of the nasal cavity.

155
00:10:00,161 --> 00:10:03,160
And then they used something like this.

156
00:10:03,161 --> 00:10:05,160
I mean, it looks like a coat hanger.

157
00:10:05,161 --> 00:10:08,160
And what they did was they inserted it through the nasal passages

158
00:10:08,161 --> 00:10:10,160
into the cranium.

159
00:10:12,160 --> 00:10:14,160
The brain doesn't stick to the tool.

160
00:10:14,161 --> 00:10:16,160
It's almost too liquid.

161
00:10:16,161 --> 00:10:20,400
So what we realized is that the way the Egyptians did it was,

162
00:10:21,040 --> 00:10:24,340
they indeed put the long coat hanger light instrument through the nose.

163
00:10:24,940 --> 00:10:27,520
But then once it's in the cranium, in the brain,

164
00:10:27,960 --> 00:10:29,880
you use it like a kitchen whisk almost.

165
00:10:29,881 --> 00:10:32,660
We rotated it around and around and around,

166
00:10:33,100 --> 00:10:35,580
breaking down the brain, liquefying it.

167
00:10:35,920 --> 00:10:37,860
Then you turn the cadaver upside down,

168
00:10:38,060 --> 00:10:40,720
and the brain runs out through the nose like a liquid.

169
00:10:41,160 --> 00:10:42,560
That's how the Egyptians did it.

170
00:10:42,561 --> 00:10:46,860
The ancient embalmers removed moisture from inside the body

171
00:10:46,861 --> 00:10:52,420
by inserting pouches of a sodium carbonate compound called natrin.

172
00:10:53,560 --> 00:10:54,700
This is natrin.

173
00:10:55,440 --> 00:10:59,700
It's exactly like the natrin the ancient Egyptian embalmers used 2,000 years ago.

174
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:03,900
Natrin is basically a salt, and the way it dehydrates is like any salt.

175
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:04,940
It's hygroscopic.

176
00:11:04,941 --> 00:11:10,260
It pulls the water out of the body, bonds with it, and the body becomes dehydrated.

177
00:11:12,560 --> 00:11:15,860
Then, Dr. Breyer copied the Egyptian practice

178
00:11:15,960 --> 00:11:19,260
of covering the outside of the body with natron

179
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:22,160
and leaving it to dry in the heat for 35 days.

180
00:11:25,300 --> 00:11:26,400
The result?

181
00:11:26,500 --> 00:11:30,020
A dried out but recognizable body.

182
00:11:30,120 --> 00:11:33,320
This, in essence, is what a mummy would have looked like thousands

183
00:11:33,420 --> 00:11:36,720
of years ago before the final and most recognizable step

184
00:11:36,820 --> 00:11:42,560
in mummification, wrapping the deceased in linen, lots of linen.

185
00:11:42,561 --> 00:11:44,780
And wrapping was a ritual.

186
00:11:45,720 --> 00:11:48,060
They wrapped each finger individually,

187
00:11:48,580 --> 00:11:50,940
the hands,

188
00:11:52,240 --> 00:11:53,900
the legs.

189
00:11:54,900 --> 00:11:57,440
There are magical spells on the bandages.

190
00:11:58,020 --> 00:12:00,880
As this is going on, priests are chanting prayers.

191
00:12:01,220 --> 00:12:03,000
So there's a ritual that's going on

192
00:12:03,001 --> 00:12:05,120
parallel to the physical process.

193
00:12:06,200 --> 00:12:08,580
Over 20 pounds of linen wrappings

194
00:12:08,581 --> 00:12:11,100
were used during a typical Egyptian mummification.

195
00:12:11,101 --> 00:12:14,520
If you were wealthy enough, as you were being wrapped,

196
00:12:15,220 --> 00:12:18,980
within the layers of wrapping, they would place magical amulets.

197
00:12:19,340 --> 00:12:22,600
Now, an amulet is anything that's small that can be worn for protection.

198
00:12:23,620 --> 00:12:27,440
So in the bandages, you could have as many as 100 amulets.

199
00:12:28,360 --> 00:12:30,580
This is an ancient amulet from a mummy.

200
00:12:30,900 --> 00:12:32,120
It's 3,000 years old.

201
00:12:32,620 --> 00:12:36,420
And if you look at it closely, you can see that it's carved like a beetle.

202
00:12:37,200 --> 00:12:39,100
And on the bottom is the payoff.

203
00:12:39,101 --> 00:12:40,600
There are two little holes.

204
00:12:41,100 --> 00:12:44,220
That's where it was sewn onto the mummy to protect it forever.

205
00:12:44,520 --> 00:12:45,520
It was in the wrappings.

206
00:12:46,340 --> 00:12:49,620
The wrappings were coated with resin made from tree sap.

207
00:12:50,920 --> 00:12:54,140
The resin hardened into a black tar-like substance,

208
00:12:54,600 --> 00:12:57,180
which ensured the wrapping stayed in place.

209
00:12:58,940 --> 00:13:02,700
The word mummy comes from the Arabic word mumia,

210
00:13:03,120 --> 00:13:04,540
which means black tar.

211
00:13:06,140 --> 00:13:10,500
The entire process took the Egyptians up to 70 days to complete.

212
00:13:11,100 --> 00:13:18,040
For these modern-day mummifiers, it was an education in a long-lost ancient art.

213
00:13:19,440 --> 00:13:21,800
I think the mummy project was really important.

214
00:13:22,540 --> 00:13:27,040
It was the first time in 2,000 years that anyone had conducted an Egyptian mummification.

215
00:13:27,740 --> 00:13:31,540
And for the first time, we know the details now of how they did it.

216
00:13:31,780 --> 00:13:33,080
So I think it was really important.

217
00:13:35,560 --> 00:13:38,480
Although the process was over for Dr. Breyer,

218
00:13:39,480 --> 00:13:44,020
The ancients still had much to do to protect the mummy for eternity.

219
00:13:46,220 --> 00:13:51,440
In addition to the wrappings, the Egyptians used a papier-mâché-like material

220
00:13:51,441 --> 00:13:55,860
called a cartonnage to cover the head and feet of the mummy.

221
00:13:57,520 --> 00:14:00,000
Then the body was placed in a wooden coffin.

222
00:14:01,220 --> 00:14:04,780
And finally, if the deceased had been wealthy or a pharaoh,

223
00:14:04,781 --> 00:14:09,780
The mummy was sealed in a stone or granite sarcophagus.

224
00:14:09,781 --> 00:14:11,780
So it's kind of like a whole set of luggage

225
00:14:11,781 --> 00:14:13,780
that fits one inside the other.

226
00:14:13,781 --> 00:14:15,780
You've got all of these different layers of protection,

227
00:14:15,781 --> 00:14:17,780
all for eternity.

228
00:14:19,780 --> 00:14:22,780
Although mummification was a complicated,

229
00:14:22,781 --> 00:14:25,780
time-consuming task, it was only the start

230
00:14:25,781 --> 00:14:28,780
of a burial ritual.

231
00:14:28,781 --> 00:14:31,780
The Egyptians honored the dead by constructing

232
00:14:31,781 --> 00:14:34,920
by constructing enormous tombs and temples,

233
00:14:34,921 --> 00:14:37,920
which became wonders of the world.

234
00:14:44,420 --> 00:14:47,240
Dr. Bob Breyer used more than 400 pounds

235
00:14:47,241 --> 00:14:51,640
of the salt compound natron to create his modern-day mummy.

236
00:14:51,641 --> 00:14:57,640
Mummy Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

237
00:14:57,641 --> 00:15:06,640
Egypt is the only country in the world where the process of preserving a body became an art form.

238
00:15:09,840 --> 00:15:14,580
The Egyptians began to develop their skills before 3000 BC.

239
00:15:16,940 --> 00:15:20,600
Mummification actually was discovered sort of by accident by the ancient Egyptians.

240
00:15:21,160 --> 00:15:24,560
This is such a hot, dry climate. The sand is virtually an oven.

241
00:15:24,561 --> 00:15:28,500
They buried people in graves in the ground

242
00:15:28,501 --> 00:15:31,860
and then discovered that these bodies were pretty much preserved,

243
00:15:32,200 --> 00:15:34,700
almost like dried fish.

244
00:15:35,140 --> 00:15:38,180
And they realized that nature could do this

245
00:15:38,181 --> 00:15:40,440
and then began to develop technologies

246
00:15:40,441 --> 00:15:42,620
and a pretty sophisticated science

247
00:15:42,621 --> 00:15:45,220
to be able to sort of artificially do the same thing.

248
00:15:46,140 --> 00:15:48,420
As it evolved over the centuries,

249
00:15:49,220 --> 00:15:51,620
mummification grew into a vast industry.

250
00:15:52,620 --> 00:15:54,180
When it came to mummification,

251
00:15:54,560 --> 00:15:57,520
The Egyptians were way ahead of everybody in the Near East.

252
00:15:58,060 --> 00:15:59,360
Nobody could do it like they did.

253
00:15:59,460 --> 00:16:00,900
They also had the resources.

254
00:16:01,440 --> 00:16:03,960
They were a wealthy country that had conquered the Near East,

255
00:16:04,100 --> 00:16:06,160
so it takes a lot of money to do this,

256
00:16:06,280 --> 00:16:07,600
and they had the money to do it,

257
00:16:07,601 --> 00:16:11,120
so they could indulge themselves and really perfect the art form.

258
00:16:12,580 --> 00:16:16,160
The Egyptians utilized their technology and resources

259
00:16:16,161 --> 00:16:17,760
not only for mummification,

260
00:16:18,280 --> 00:16:21,460
but also to build enormous tombs.

261
00:16:21,461 --> 00:16:28,460
A vast complex of these tombs is known as a necropolis, or city of the dead.

262
00:16:28,461 --> 00:16:35,460
The design and technology to make the tombs was pretty sophisticated and pretty demanding.

263
00:16:35,461 --> 00:16:40,460
They were working with bronze tools, hewing out square chambers in the rock,

264
00:16:40,461 --> 00:16:44,460
sometimes hundreds of yards deep into the limestone.

265
00:16:44,461 --> 00:16:49,460
And typically, a pharaoh had this team working for 40 years to prepare his tomb.

266
00:16:49,461 --> 00:16:52,860
That's a remarkable feat of skill and engineering,

267
00:16:53,260 --> 00:16:54,700
considering what they had at their disposal.

268
00:16:59,480 --> 00:17:01,600
The Great Pyramid of Khufu,

269
00:17:01,900 --> 00:17:03,540
one of the seven wonders of the world,

270
00:17:04,100 --> 00:17:06,980
is a tomb constructed utilizing this organization

271
00:17:06,981 --> 00:17:08,320
and building technology.

272
00:17:11,820 --> 00:17:16,860
It is still the single largest masonry building ever constructed,

273
00:17:16,861 --> 00:17:19,860
And all of that was done to house, in the center,

274
00:17:20,060 --> 00:17:23,540
a little tiny burial chamber with the pharaoh's mummy inside.

275
00:17:26,680 --> 00:17:30,820
The Egyptians believed that a dead person's soul left the body,

276
00:17:31,280 --> 00:17:34,320
but would rejoin it at burial.

277
00:17:35,940 --> 00:17:40,220
So the deceased needed his body and worldly possessions for the afterlife.

278
00:17:42,500 --> 00:17:45,640
The basic design for most Egyptian tombs

279
00:17:45,641 --> 00:17:47,740
included three essential compartments.

280
00:17:48,520 --> 00:17:51,260
The entrance, the offering chamber,

281
00:17:51,820 --> 00:17:53,280
and the burial chamber.

282
00:17:54,420 --> 00:17:57,840
This is a typical entrance to an ancient Egyptian tomb.

283
00:17:58,720 --> 00:18:00,300
These hieroglyphs would tell you

284
00:18:00,301 --> 00:18:02,320
who was buried in this tomb,

285
00:18:02,860 --> 00:18:06,100
would also present a welcome and a curse.

286
00:18:06,880 --> 00:18:09,800
The curse or the warning in the hieroglyph says,

287
00:18:09,801 --> 00:18:11,920
if you come with bad intentions,

288
00:18:12,400 --> 00:18:14,600
depart now before it is too late.

289
00:18:14,601 --> 00:18:17,500
But we have good intentions, so let's go inside.

290
00:18:25,720 --> 00:18:30,660
Tomb builders carved a tunnel that connected the entrance to the first room inside,

291
00:18:31,680 --> 00:18:33,280
the offering chamber.

292
00:18:34,960 --> 00:18:38,860
A door like this would have been found in an ancient tomb.

293
00:18:38,861 --> 00:18:44,680
The relatives of the deceased person would leave offerings here at the bottom of the door,

294
00:18:45,040 --> 00:18:50,560
and the deceased person's soul would magically enter here

295
00:18:50,561 --> 00:18:54,920
and take the food or other offerings to the afterlife,

296
00:18:55,440 --> 00:18:57,580
back out through this same magical door.

297
00:19:02,940 --> 00:19:07,280
The Egyptians dug a shaft, sometimes hundreds of feet long,

298
00:19:07,281 --> 00:19:09,600
that connected to the final chamber.

299
00:19:12,120 --> 00:19:14,280
We're in the burial chamber now.

300
00:19:14,700 --> 00:19:17,060
This is the granite sarcophagus.

301
00:19:17,660 --> 00:19:20,740
Inside of this would have been a wooden sarcophagus,

302
00:19:20,880 --> 00:19:23,880
and inside of that would have been placed the body of the tomb owner.

303
00:19:24,360 --> 00:19:27,100
All of this, then, would have been buried underground.

304
00:19:28,080 --> 00:19:31,840
The hieroglyphs and images in the burial chamber

305
00:19:31,841 --> 00:19:36,500
describe the life that the tomb owner wanted to live in the afterlife.

306
00:19:37,280 --> 00:19:40,280
Included in the burial chamber would have been items

307
00:19:40,380 --> 00:19:44,180
that the deceased person would hope to use in the next life,

308
00:19:44,280 --> 00:19:49,320
treasures that they would take with them to the afterlife.

309
00:19:49,420 --> 00:19:52,220
The enormous treasures became easy targets

310
00:19:52,320 --> 00:19:54,160
for grave robbers.

311
00:19:54,260 --> 00:19:56,760
So tomb builders created a barricade

312
00:19:56,860 --> 00:20:00,240
to protect these riches by backfilling the passages

313
00:20:00,340 --> 00:20:02,440
to the burial chamber.

314
00:20:02,540 --> 00:20:04,180
So the first thing a thief encountered

315
00:20:04,181 --> 00:20:08,180
when he found the tomb site was sometimes 300 yards

316
00:20:08,220 --> 00:20:11,180
of solid stone rubble that he had to dig through

317
00:20:11,220 --> 00:20:14,180
to get into the tomb proper.

318
00:20:14,220 --> 00:20:16,860
Over the centuries, the interiors of tombs

319
00:20:16,900 --> 00:20:19,300
became increasingly elaborate,

320
00:20:19,320 --> 00:20:22,900
with the intention of confusing tomb raiders.

321
00:20:22,940 --> 00:20:26,260
Often, Egyptian tombs were designed specifically

322
00:20:26,300 --> 00:20:30,700
to hide the mummy, to keep the mummy secret.

323
00:20:30,701 --> 00:20:38,740
So one of the manners was to use false passages, false doors, false chambers, etc.,

324
00:20:38,741 --> 00:20:43,100
so that people would be lost, in effect, kind of in a maze, often.

325
00:20:44,180 --> 00:20:46,920
However, this did little to deter grave robbing.

326
00:20:49,160 --> 00:20:52,320
Thieves often desecrated the mummies and their tombs

327
00:20:52,321 --> 00:20:55,820
to steal the riches that were meant for the afterlife.

328
00:20:55,821 --> 00:21:00,520
The effect of the plundering of the tomb over the centuries

329
00:21:00,521 --> 00:21:04,120
was that an untold number of ancient riches

330
00:21:04,121 --> 00:21:06,740
have been lost or dispersed.

331
00:21:07,540 --> 00:21:10,980
A threat greater than grave robbing was yet to come.

332
00:21:12,900 --> 00:21:17,320
In 525 B.C., the Persians conquered Egypt,

333
00:21:18,380 --> 00:21:21,220
followed by the Greeks and the Romans.

334
00:21:21,221 --> 00:21:25,800
The invaders were fascinated by mummification

335
00:21:25,801 --> 00:21:28,640
and allowed the practice to continue.

336
00:21:30,560 --> 00:21:35,460
But later in 642 AD, the Arabs occupied Egypt

337
00:21:35,461 --> 00:21:37,940
and Islam became the dominant religion.

338
00:21:39,920 --> 00:21:43,540
Mummification was not tolerated and came to an end.

339
00:21:44,760 --> 00:21:46,620
And then it became a lost art.

340
00:21:47,420 --> 00:21:49,640
Egypt was eventually invaded by the Arabs,

341
00:21:49,641 --> 00:21:51,680
becomes a Muslim country,

342
00:21:52,180 --> 00:21:55,060
and in a sense is cut off from the Christian West.

343
00:21:55,460 --> 00:21:57,740
So throughout the Middle Ages, throughout the Renaissance,

344
00:21:58,140 --> 00:21:59,480
we've lost touch with Egypt,

345
00:21:59,740 --> 00:22:01,640
and that art of mummification is gone.

346
00:22:02,540 --> 00:22:03,920
So sands come in,

347
00:22:04,620 --> 00:22:06,780
whole temples are lost,

348
00:22:07,380 --> 00:22:08,680
tombs are buried under sand,

349
00:22:08,681 --> 00:22:10,480
and it simply remains there,

350
00:22:10,860 --> 00:22:12,560
waiting for archaeologists to discover it.

351
00:22:15,240 --> 00:22:18,260
With the advance of another conquering superpower,

352
00:22:18,261 --> 00:22:21,360
Egypt's mummies would be rediscovered.

353
00:22:22,660 --> 00:22:26,000
In 1798, Napoleon invaded Egypt

354
00:22:26,001 --> 00:22:29,700
and took scientists with him to document Egyptian culture.

355
00:22:30,640 --> 00:22:34,280
And they really do the first archaeological survey of Egypt.

356
00:22:34,720 --> 00:22:38,000
And this is how the West really learns about Egypt for the first time.

357
00:22:38,320 --> 00:22:41,580
When they start publishing their drawings and their paintings

358
00:22:41,581 --> 00:22:45,440
and people see for the first time accurate depictions of mummies,

359
00:22:45,441 --> 00:22:49,440
This is when Europe goes wild with Egyptomania.

360
00:22:49,441 --> 00:22:52,440
In the 19th and early 20th centuries,

361
00:22:52,441 --> 00:22:55,440
Egyptian mummies became historical treasures

362
00:22:55,441 --> 00:22:58,440
sought after by museums worldwide.

363
00:22:58,441 --> 00:23:01,440
Once people realize how important mummies are,

364
00:23:01,441 --> 00:23:03,440
that they're part of history,

365
00:23:03,441 --> 00:23:05,440
then they start being displayed in museums,

366
00:23:05,441 --> 00:23:07,440
being taken care of,

367
00:23:07,441 --> 00:23:10,440
and then that leads to studying them.

368
00:23:10,441 --> 00:23:12,440
During the 1900s,

369
00:23:12,441 --> 00:23:15,240
archaeologists combed Egyptian burial sites

370
00:23:15,340 --> 00:23:18,380
for these links to the past.

371
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:20,080
One of the most important discoveries

372
00:23:20,180 --> 00:23:23,580
was the tomb of King Tut.

373
00:23:23,680 --> 00:23:25,980
But there was more than treasure here.

374
00:23:26,080 --> 00:23:29,100
There was a curse and a murder mystery.

375
00:23:33,800 --> 00:23:38,700
The Egyptians mummified not only humans, but also animals.

376
00:23:38,701 --> 00:23:42,500
Dogs, cats, cows, and even lions

377
00:23:42,501 --> 00:23:45,600
have been discovered mummified in ancient tombs.

378
00:23:47,460 --> 00:23:49,900
Mummy Tech will return on Modern Marvel,

379
00:23:50,700 --> 00:23:52,860
here on the History Channel.

380
00:23:55,460 --> 00:24:00,000
King Tutankhamen, or as he is best known, King Tut,

381
00:24:00,660 --> 00:24:03,660
is probably the most famous mummy in the world.

382
00:24:05,560 --> 00:24:06,260
Hi.

383
00:24:06,261 --> 00:24:10,380
The pharaoh is not only celebrated for the enormous riches

384
00:24:10,381 --> 00:24:15,660
discovered in his tomb, but also for the mysteries surrounding

385
00:24:15,661 --> 00:24:17,440
his life and death.

386
00:24:19,320 --> 00:24:23,080
Today's technology has attempted to uncover some of the answers

387
00:24:23,081 --> 00:24:25,160
to this legendary intrigue.

388
00:24:27,980 --> 00:24:31,720
Fascination has surrounded Tut since British archaeologist

389
00:24:31,721 --> 00:24:35,180
Howard Carter first discovered him in 1922.

390
00:24:36,240 --> 00:24:42,380
When he discovered the tomb and actually began to publicize these finds, this news galloped around the world.

391
00:24:42,520 --> 00:24:48,920
I mean, front page news in New York and in Sydney and in Cairo and in Paris were all talking about this sensational find.

392
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:49,980
It really was Tutmania.

393
00:24:51,300 --> 00:24:56,700
In the beginning, Tutmania was motivated by the stores of riches found in his tomb.

394
00:24:58,360 --> 00:25:04,040
There were over 5,000 objects put into this very small tomb with King Tut.

395
00:25:04,041 --> 00:25:06,560
There's gold, there's alabaster, there's ivory,

396
00:25:07,100 --> 00:25:10,000
jewelry and precious stones that still dazzle your eye.

397
00:25:10,340 --> 00:25:13,380
And one of the most remarkable things is how well-preserved they are,

398
00:25:13,500 --> 00:25:14,800
as though they were made yesterday.

399
00:25:16,700 --> 00:25:19,440
One of the most celebrated relics unearthed

400
00:25:19,441 --> 00:25:22,880
was the gold burial mask that was placed on Tut's face.

401
00:25:24,260 --> 00:25:28,580
This particular death mask is one of the most spectacular objects

402
00:25:28,581 --> 00:25:32,040
ever found in anywhere, anywhere and from any culture,

403
00:25:32,041 --> 00:25:36,720
made of gold and a variety of other metals, including silver,

404
00:25:37,040 --> 00:25:40,620
but also with stones such as onyx, turquoise, obsidian,

405
00:25:40,780 --> 00:25:42,860
a variety of different elements put together

406
00:25:42,861 --> 00:25:46,300
to make an absolutely gorgeous death mask.

407
00:25:46,720 --> 00:25:51,700
In a short time, supernatural mystery would accompany this sublime treasure.

408
00:25:53,380 --> 00:25:56,900
Legend has it that those who entered the tomb became ill

409
00:25:56,901 --> 00:25:59,000
and died shortly thereafter.

410
00:25:59,001 --> 00:26:04,560
The first individual to die that started the legend of the curse of the mummy was Lord Carnarvon.

411
00:26:04,920 --> 00:26:07,700
He was the man who financed Carter's digs.

412
00:26:08,060 --> 00:26:12,980
He died about two months after Carter opened the chamber that had the mummy in it.

413
00:26:13,420 --> 00:26:21,480
And so the legend was born, and then over time, anybody who was associated with the project who died suddenly was ascribed to be a victim of the mummy's curse.

414
00:26:22,660 --> 00:26:24,760
For decades, the myth endured.

415
00:26:26,040 --> 00:26:28,040
Hollywood even cashed in on the curse.

416
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:31,740
I think the mummy, as a horror for you,

417
00:26:31,760 --> 00:26:35,500
really becomes prominent after the discovery of King Tut's tomb.

418
00:26:35,540 --> 00:26:38,380
There was all this talk about the curse of King Tut,

419
00:26:38,400 --> 00:26:41,940
and then you get the idea of mummies as things that kill people.

420
00:26:41,980 --> 00:26:44,780
So I think it's really after Tutankhamun's tomb was discovered.

421
00:26:44,820 --> 00:26:49,360
The curse may have struck gold at the box office,

422
00:26:49,380 --> 00:26:51,960
but science has some far different theories

423
00:26:51,980 --> 00:26:55,400
about what might cause death to those who enter tombs.

424
00:26:55,401 --> 00:27:02,740
there apparently are some very real scientific reasons that are surfacing now

425
00:27:02,741 --> 00:27:07,420
most archaeologists these days who open sealed tombs in ancient egypt actually let the tombs

426
00:27:07,421 --> 00:27:11,840
breathe so to speak for a few days because there may well be build-ups of toxic gases

427
00:27:11,841 --> 00:27:16,440
other toxic materials these are chambers that have been sealed for countless generations

428
00:27:16,441 --> 00:27:23,340
they have decomposing bodies in them foodstuffs the resins and the other chemicals that were

429
00:27:23,341 --> 00:27:26,100
made and used during the lummification process,

430
00:27:26,240 --> 00:27:29,180
all of that can contribute to a pretty risky environment.

431
00:27:29,280 --> 00:27:31,280
So archaeologists actually let the tombs breathe

432
00:27:31,380 --> 00:27:33,280
before they go in.

433
00:27:33,380 --> 00:27:35,780
So perhaps science has solved the mystery

434
00:27:35,880 --> 00:27:39,380
of the curse of King Tut.

435
00:27:39,480 --> 00:27:41,500
Likewise, science has been put to use

436
00:27:41,600 --> 00:27:49,200
to solve another enduring King Tut mystery, a royal murder.

437
00:27:49,300 --> 00:27:51,100
The mystery of King Tut's death

438
00:27:51,101 --> 00:27:54,480
began at the time that he was found,

439
00:27:54,760 --> 00:27:56,820
because they discovered that he was a youth,

440
00:27:56,980 --> 00:27:59,600
and we needed to find out exactly how he died.

441
00:27:59,680 --> 00:28:00,900
Did he die a natural death?

442
00:28:01,100 --> 00:28:02,840
Was he murdered? Was he killed?

443
00:28:03,360 --> 00:28:07,820
The boy king ruled ancient Egypt about 3,500 years ago

444
00:28:07,821 --> 00:28:10,440
after inheriting the throne from his father

445
00:28:10,441 --> 00:28:12,100
at the tender age of nine.

446
00:28:13,960 --> 00:28:17,180
Ten years later, his reign was cut short.

447
00:28:17,780 --> 00:28:20,080
And there were a number of contenders for the throne,

448
00:28:20,081 --> 00:28:22,720
so it's certainly within the realm of possibility.

449
00:28:23,020 --> 00:28:26,940
We know that there were other assassinations in Egyptian history of pharaohs,

450
00:28:26,980 --> 00:28:30,900
so it would certainly be possible that Tutankhamen could have been murdered.

451
00:28:31,500 --> 00:28:36,860
In an effort to solve the mystery, Tut's mummy was X-rayed in 1968,

452
00:28:37,280 --> 00:28:41,720
which revealed an intriguing hole in the back of his head.

453
00:28:43,520 --> 00:28:46,820
The idea that Tut might have died in a palace coup

454
00:28:46,821 --> 00:28:50,860
really took hold after the X-ray of the mummy

455
00:28:50,861 --> 00:28:53,760
and revealed a chip of bone driven up into the back of his head.

456
00:28:53,820 --> 00:28:54,880
He might have been bludgeoned.

457
00:28:57,500 --> 00:29:02,000
In 2005, Tuts remains underwent a CT scan,

458
00:29:02,400 --> 00:29:05,560
which produced detailed cross-section images.

459
00:29:06,380 --> 00:29:10,160
The discovery in the CAT scan about the hole in the back of the head

460
00:29:10,161 --> 00:29:13,720
indicated that it was part of the mummification process.

461
00:29:13,721 --> 00:29:19,160
In fact, in the 18th dynasty, many of the mummies had holes in the back of the head,

462
00:29:19,220 --> 00:29:21,260
they've discovered when they do these CAT scans.

463
00:29:21,640 --> 00:29:24,720
So what caused the boy king to die so young?

464
00:29:26,480 --> 00:29:31,160
The CT scan may have also revealed the real cause of Tut's demise.

465
00:29:33,280 --> 00:29:41,740
The latest discoveries with CT scanning seem to have suggested that there was perhaps a fracture in his leg

466
00:29:41,741 --> 00:29:45,800
and that perhaps an infection that set in later could have killed him,

467
00:29:46,080 --> 00:29:49,500
we know that even a toothache, the cavity,

468
00:29:49,760 --> 00:29:52,820
could eventually lead to someone's death.

469
00:29:52,900 --> 00:29:58,020
And in fact, some of the pharaohs seem to have died from infected teeth.

470
00:29:58,680 --> 00:30:03,260
This new evidence has essentially ended the investigation into Tut's murder.

471
00:30:04,620 --> 00:30:06,680
The case for murder is closed.

472
00:30:06,681 --> 00:30:11,200
100% of the population of Egyptologists believe he was not murdered.

473
00:30:11,740 --> 00:30:16,140
In addition to finding the cause of King Tut's death,

474
00:30:16,141 --> 00:30:21,860
technology has also given us clues as to what he looked like.

475
00:30:21,861 --> 00:30:26,720
Using facial reconstruction, much like law enforcement uses today

476
00:30:26,721 --> 00:30:29,980
to help solve murder cases, scientists

477
00:30:29,981 --> 00:30:32,540
have created a lifelike model of the Egyptian king.

478
00:30:35,800 --> 00:30:38,400
The CT scan images were used to construct

479
00:30:38,401 --> 00:30:42,540
plastic model of Tut's skull.

480
00:30:42,541 --> 00:30:46,700
Then, using clay, a sculptor molded layers of musculature

481
00:30:46,701 --> 00:30:48,540
and skin.

482
00:30:48,541 --> 00:30:53,780
An artist fleshed out details, eyebrow thickness, nose, lips,

483
00:30:53,781 --> 00:30:56,300
and the approximate shape of Tut's ears.

484
00:30:56,301 --> 00:31:00,000
Finally, glass eyes, hair, and even historically accurate

485
00:31:00,001 --> 00:31:02,460
makeup completed the reconstruction.

486
00:31:02,461 --> 00:31:09,460
This is believed to be what King Tut actually looked like.

487
00:31:09,461 --> 00:31:16,460
I think because of the technology that underlies this reconstruction,

488
00:31:16,461 --> 00:31:22,460
it's as close as anybody is going to get to what Tut looked like in life.

489
00:31:22,461 --> 00:31:28,460
However, the study of the boy king's remains may be far from over.

490
00:31:28,461 --> 00:31:34,000
from over. Can technology reveal even more?

491
00:31:34,001 --> 00:31:38,900
The fascination of Tut endures and I think as new technologies evolve, he will be a natural

492
00:31:38,901 --> 00:31:44,020
subject to apply those new technologies and unlock pieces of the story we can't even guess

493
00:31:44,021 --> 00:31:47,560
at yet. King Tut has always received the finest

494
00:31:47,561 --> 00:31:50,339
care and scientific study.

495
00:31:50,340 --> 00:31:56,860
But for centuries, countless mummies were desecrated, stolen,

496
00:31:56,861 --> 00:31:58,860
or simply lost and forgotten.

497
00:32:03,260 --> 00:32:05,860
The treasure found in King Tut's tomb

498
00:32:05,861 --> 00:32:09,860
is valued today at more than a half billion dollars.

499
00:32:11,460 --> 00:32:14,860
Mummy Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

500
00:32:21,660 --> 00:32:25,360
Had the ancient Egyptians known the degree of shocking desecration

501
00:32:25,361 --> 00:32:28,960
to which their mummies would be subject.

502
00:32:28,961 --> 00:32:34,400
They might have thought twice about embalming them for eternity.

503
00:32:34,401 --> 00:32:38,280
Through the ages, mummies have been used for all kinds of things.

504
00:32:38,281 --> 00:32:42,820
People believed that ground-up mummy itself could give you immortality.

505
00:32:42,821 --> 00:32:47,320
So apothecaries in Europe, pharmacists, started grinding up mummies

506
00:32:47,321 --> 00:32:51,460
and using it as a medical ingredient to help the sick.

507
00:32:51,461 --> 00:32:54,520
During the Civil War, there was a shortage of paper.

508
00:32:54,521 --> 00:32:57,040
So the linen from mummies, the mummy bandages,

509
00:32:57,320 --> 00:33:00,000
were shipped over to America and pulped

510
00:33:00,001 --> 00:33:03,120
and made into paper for various industries.

511
00:33:04,440 --> 00:33:07,320
19th century aristocrats entertained themselves

512
00:33:07,321 --> 00:33:10,280
by buying mummies and holding what were called

513
00:33:10,281 --> 00:33:13,280
observation sessions for public audiences.

514
00:33:15,500 --> 00:33:16,980
People would unwrap them,

515
00:33:17,300 --> 00:33:19,180
and they'd want to see what's inside the bandages.

516
00:33:19,181 --> 00:33:21,240
They'd certainly want to see what the face looked like.

517
00:33:21,580 --> 00:33:23,260
And really, we've lost a lot of knowledge

518
00:33:23,261 --> 00:33:24,640
and information about that.

519
00:33:24,720 --> 00:33:26,500
I mean, I think if any poor ancient Egyptian

520
00:33:26,501 --> 00:33:27,940
knew what was going to happen to his mummy that way,

521
00:33:27,980 --> 00:33:29,440
I think he'd be very unhappy about it.

522
00:33:30,060 --> 00:33:32,600
This poor fellow may have been the guest of honor,

523
00:33:33,580 --> 00:33:37,120
purchased for an unwrapping party held in a private home.

524
00:33:38,160 --> 00:33:41,320
It's very probable that this mummy was unwrapped

525
00:33:41,321 --> 00:33:45,320
during Victorian times as part of one of those parlor games

526
00:33:45,321 --> 00:33:47,700
where they would just unwrap the linens

527
00:33:47,701 --> 00:33:50,280
to see what amulets or jewels or treasures

528
00:33:50,281 --> 00:33:52,100
would fall off of the mummy's body.

529
00:33:52,101 --> 00:33:54,100
They were fascinated with what they might find.

530
00:33:54,200 --> 00:33:55,100
It was a treasure hunt.

531
00:33:55,200 --> 00:33:57,500
It was a lot easier than going to Egypt

532
00:33:57,600 --> 00:33:58,800
and digging through the sand.

533
00:33:58,900 --> 00:34:00,900
They could simply unwrap these linens

534
00:34:01,000 --> 00:34:04,540
and see what would fall out, and then it belonged to them.

535
00:34:04,640 --> 00:34:08,040
Mummies were bought and sold like any other commodity.

536
00:34:08,140 --> 00:34:09,960
Many were lost over time.

537
00:34:12,060 --> 00:34:15,560
In 1860, a collection of mummies was purchased

538
00:34:15,660 --> 00:34:17,660
by a museum in Niagara Falls.

539
00:34:17,661 --> 00:34:21,660
No one knew that in that collection rested one of the

540
00:34:21,661 --> 00:34:26,660
most powerful men ever to walk the banks of the Nile.

541
00:34:26,661 --> 00:34:31,660
The Niagara Falls Museum was really less of a true museum

542
00:34:31,661 --> 00:34:36,660
than a kind of sideshow, where it had two-headed animals and

543
00:34:36,661 --> 00:34:40,660
the barrels people went over the falls in and kind of bizarre

544
00:34:40,661 --> 00:34:44,660
things like that. But one mummy in this collection

545
00:34:44,661 --> 00:34:48,900
a collection of oddities seemed to stand out.

546
00:34:48,901 --> 00:34:50,760
As a seven-year-old or eight-year-old boy,

547
00:34:50,761 --> 00:34:52,460
I went to the museum and saw this mummy

548
00:34:52,461 --> 00:34:54,340
and stood face to face, if you will,

549
00:34:54,341 --> 00:34:56,100
with this ancient person.

550
00:34:56,101 --> 00:34:57,840
The first thing that made people look at this

551
00:34:57,841 --> 00:34:59,700
was the pose of the mummy

552
00:34:59,701 --> 00:35:03,240
and the obvious high quality of the process.

553
00:35:03,241 --> 00:35:06,820
The real tip-off was that the position of the arms

554
00:35:06,821 --> 00:35:08,820
was crossed over the chest.

555
00:35:08,821 --> 00:35:12,560
This was a posture reserved only for pharaohs.

556
00:35:12,561 --> 00:35:14,460
For more than 100 years,

557
00:35:14,461 --> 00:35:17,400
This mummy entertained the masses,

558
00:35:17,401 --> 00:35:22,560
until the Niagara Falls Museum closed in 1998.

559
00:35:22,561 --> 00:35:25,620
Its mummies were sold to the Michael C. Carlos Museum

560
00:35:25,621 --> 00:35:27,220
at Emory University in Atlanta.

561
00:35:30,280 --> 00:35:32,820
There, the mysterious cross-armed body

562
00:35:32,821 --> 00:35:35,780
finally received the high-tech attention needed

563
00:35:35,781 --> 00:35:39,240
for a thorough background check.

564
00:35:39,241 --> 00:35:42,320
So once it got here, we began consulting

565
00:35:42,321 --> 00:35:44,820
with experts from all over the world.

566
00:35:44,821 --> 00:35:46,480
However, the pharaoh connection

567
00:35:46,481 --> 00:35:49,020
couldn't be established with DNA,

568
00:35:49,021 --> 00:35:51,080
since Egyptian authorities were reluctant

569
00:35:51,081 --> 00:35:54,320
to give samples from their royal mummies.

570
00:35:54,321 --> 00:35:58,500
Instead, non-invasive CT scans and x-rays

571
00:35:58,501 --> 00:36:02,100
were compared to other known ancient pharaohs.

572
00:36:02,101 --> 00:36:04,820
Just who was this mummy?

573
00:36:04,821 --> 00:36:10,560
Stunningly, one candidate stood out, Ramsey's the first.

574
00:36:10,561 --> 00:36:17,560
The style of the wrappings matched other important royal mummies, most closely that of Setti I

575
00:36:17,561 --> 00:36:25,560
and Ramses II, son and grandson of the missing mummy of Ramses I.

576
00:36:25,561 --> 00:36:34,560
The CT scan was very useful because it revealed inside the mummy two very tightly coiled pieces

577
00:36:34,561 --> 00:36:41,100
of linen in the body cavity used as filling to help sort of plump out the body once the

578
00:36:41,101 --> 00:36:43,272
internal organs were removed.

579
00:36:43,273 --> 00:36:48,140
This is paralleled only by the mummy of Seti I, the son of Ramses

580
00:36:48,141 --> 00:36:52,104
I. So that seemed to clinch father and son together.

581
00:36:52,105 --> 00:36:54,620
We came to the conclusion that it

582
00:36:54,621 --> 00:36:58,104
by far was most likely the mummy of Ramses I.

583
00:36:58,105 --> 00:37:01,820
The discovery of Ramses I filled a long-standing

584
00:37:01,821 --> 00:37:07,760
gap in the historical lineage of Egyptian pharaohs.

585
00:37:07,761 --> 00:37:13,460
In order for the great king to take his place with other royal mummies,

586
00:37:13,461 --> 00:37:19,780
the Carlos Museum voluntarily sent him back to Egypt,

587
00:37:19,781 --> 00:37:23,780
where he received a regal reception.

588
00:37:31,820 --> 00:37:37,040
Without this technology, we never would have been able to make that determination.

589
00:37:37,860 --> 00:37:41,940
Being the mummy of Ramses I, giving it back its identity.

590
00:37:45,780 --> 00:37:50,200
Just as scientists use technology to give Ramses his name and his honor,

591
00:37:51,580 --> 00:37:57,340
other scientists use technology to preserve and protect legions of unnamed mummies,

592
00:37:57,780 --> 00:38:01,200
or at risk of literally crumbling to dust.

593
00:38:01,820 --> 00:38:11,820
Over a 3,000 year period, more than 70 million Egyptians were mummified.

594
00:38:11,821 --> 00:38:16,820
Mummy Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

595
00:38:16,821 --> 00:38:30,821
Locked away in sealed tombs, mummies have survived for thousands of years.

596
00:38:35,760 --> 00:38:39,980
But now that they're out in the modern world, how will they fare?

597
00:38:39,981 --> 00:38:46,700
Restoring and preserving mummies is kind of in its infancy.

598
00:38:47,080 --> 00:38:54,840
The biggest challenge that we face with the mummies is that the linens and the bodies themselves have become fragile and brittle and often broken over time.

599
00:38:56,060 --> 00:39:01,560
Preservationists use a sheer nylon material to help keep mummies from literally falling to pieces.

600
00:39:02,500 --> 00:39:06,880
This newly developed technique holds the bandages and body parts together.

601
00:39:06,881 --> 00:39:13,780
This mummy has been prepared for display in the galleries by first carefully arranging

602
00:39:13,781 --> 00:39:19,160
the wrappings that are on the mummy and then covering the whole mummy with a sheer fabric

603
00:39:19,161 --> 00:39:24,120
that matches the color of the old linen and allows you to see through it, enabling you

604
00:39:24,121 --> 00:39:28,660
to see the wrappings as they were originally arranged, somewhat as a hairnet does.

605
00:39:28,661 --> 00:39:34,640
It allows those loose and now very brittle linens to stay in place around the mummy as

606
00:39:34,641 --> 00:39:36,640
it lies on display.

607
00:39:36,641 --> 00:39:40,280
The natrin, or salt compound, that was used by the Egyptians

608
00:39:40,281 --> 00:39:44,080
to preserve mummies today may cause a mummy to actually

609
00:39:44,081 --> 00:39:44,840
disintegrate.

610
00:39:46,760 --> 00:39:49,920
And if there are any changes in temperature and humidity,

611
00:39:50,300 --> 00:39:54,060
the salt that is naturally dissolved in the material

612
00:39:54,061 --> 00:39:57,960
will re-dissolve and migrate in or out,

613
00:39:58,080 --> 00:40:01,760
depending on if the air is drier or wetter than the object.

614
00:40:02,240 --> 00:40:04,760
And the movement of this salt back and forth

615
00:40:04,761 --> 00:40:06,540
actually erodes the surface.

616
00:40:06,700 --> 00:40:09,940
It actually causes things to explode from the inside out.

617
00:40:10,780 --> 00:40:12,240
To control this problem,

618
00:40:12,940 --> 00:40:16,560
a specially designed computerized system at the Carlos Museum

619
00:40:16,561 --> 00:40:18,820
regulates temperature and humidity.

620
00:40:21,120 --> 00:40:25,160
The museum is kept at very specific environmental conditions,

621
00:40:25,161 --> 00:40:26,840
and in order to accomplish that,

622
00:40:26,940 --> 00:40:30,520
we use environmental controls through an air conditioning system

623
00:40:30,521 --> 00:40:34,020
that are set to maintain temperature at about 70 degrees

624
00:40:34,021 --> 00:40:36,340
plus or minus 2 degrees Fahrenheit,

625
00:40:36,720 --> 00:40:39,680
and then we set the relative humidity around 50%,

626
00:40:39,681 --> 00:40:41,940
plus or minus 5% RH.

627
00:40:43,720 --> 00:40:47,380
Tiny organic microbes can also wreak havoc on a mummy.

628
00:40:49,780 --> 00:40:52,140
At the Egyptian Museum in Cairo,

629
00:40:52,760 --> 00:40:57,940
a common intruder was found resting alongside kings and queens.

630
00:40:59,320 --> 00:41:02,500
It was discovered that the mummies in the Egyptian Museum in Cairo

631
00:41:02,501 --> 00:41:03,840
were growing mold.

632
00:41:04,020 --> 00:41:05,260
They were literally getting moldy.

633
00:41:06,240 --> 00:41:10,440
Everyday household mold and fungus can destroy the most revered pharaoh.

634
00:41:13,160 --> 00:41:18,620
If mummy is not protected, mummy could be disintegrated in a matter of a couple of years.

635
00:41:19,820 --> 00:41:25,480
Shin Maikawa is a senior scientist at the Getty Conservation Institute in Los Angeles, California.

636
00:41:27,860 --> 00:41:32,380
He was asked by the Cairo Museum to help solve the problem of the moldy mummies.

637
00:41:34,020 --> 00:41:41,020
Three things affecting mummies are temperature, relative humidity and oxygen.

638
00:41:41,021 --> 00:41:47,020
Oxygen is one of the largest contributors to the breakdown of organic material

639
00:41:47,021 --> 00:41:54,020
because it provides the fuel that allows microbes like mold and fungi to breed and multiply.

640
00:41:54,021 --> 00:42:00,020
To attack the problem, Maikawa designed an oxygen-free display case.

641
00:42:00,021 --> 00:42:04,020
This is a small prototype of the custom-built case.

642
00:42:06,020 --> 00:42:14,020
The goal of the display case is to provide stable, dry environment for mummies without oxygen,

643
00:42:14,021 --> 00:42:22,020
so that we can eliminate both stress from humidity variation and possibility of fungi attack.

644
00:42:22,021 --> 00:42:29,020
To create an environment good for mummies and bad for microbes, three additional materials are needed.

645
00:42:30,020 --> 00:42:40,060
A compound of essentially powdered iron absorbs oxygen in the air, while activated charcoal

646
00:42:40,061 --> 00:42:43,180
granules filter out impurities and pollutants.

647
00:42:44,640 --> 00:42:49,820
And silicon gel caplets help control the humidity or moisture.

648
00:42:51,580 --> 00:42:54,460
They're all placed inside the display case.

649
00:42:55,580 --> 00:42:58,780
Then the case is sealed, airtight.

650
00:43:00,020 --> 00:43:04,060
Now the environment of the case is made inhospitable to microbes.

651
00:43:08,060 --> 00:43:13,960
As oxygen is removed, and pure nitrogen is pumped in.

652
00:43:15,220 --> 00:43:19,460
Nothing live can possibly survive in nitrogen.

653
00:43:19,840 --> 00:43:22,780
So once you put a mummy in there, in an atmosphere of nitrogen,

654
00:43:23,120 --> 00:43:27,440
no fungus, no bacteria, no molds, it'll be preserved just perfectly.

655
00:43:28,300 --> 00:43:31,820
The Egyptian museum has now placed their royal mummies

656
00:43:31,821 --> 00:43:33,940
in Maikawa's specially designed cases.

657
00:43:35,140 --> 00:43:39,000
And the common mold problem has been vanquished.

658
00:43:40,220 --> 00:43:44,100
This play case is very easy to build, very inexpensive,

659
00:43:44,760 --> 00:43:46,860
and we can keep the mummy forever.

660
00:43:50,060 --> 00:43:54,820
And after all, forever was the goal of the ancient Egyptians

661
00:43:54,821 --> 00:43:55,880
in the first place.

662
00:43:57,440 --> 00:44:05,480
They believed that speaking the names of the dead and visiting their remains would give

663
00:44:05,481 --> 00:44:09,280
the deceased an eternal afterlife.

664
00:44:09,281 --> 00:44:18,320
Today, with the public intensely interested in mummies, it appears they may have succeeded.

